<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=seismoscope">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seismoscope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">132</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Houfeng Didong Yi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A seismoscope is an instrument that shows that the ground has shaken, without the continuous record that a seismometer keeps. The earliest well-documented one was made in 132 CE by Zhang Heng of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and called Houfeng Didong Yi. According to the History of the Later Han Dynasty, it was a bronze vessel about 2 metres across, with eight dragon's heads that dropped a ball into a bronze toad to show the direction of a quake. Mercury and pendulum seismoscopes followed in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the modern seismometer was developed in the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>132 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Zhang Heng · <a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Unverified claims that a device resembling an earthquake detector existed in Ancient Egypt have no confirmation in reliable historical sources. How the central column inside the vessel opened only one dragon's mouth is not known.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=seismoscope">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
